  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Body Proportions**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the vertical proportions. The minimum Y value is at 11.3% from the top. Elbows cluster between 70% and 82% of the total height. Chest and shoulders appear to be situated at approximately 45% to 60% of the height. I\'m focusing on these ratios to model the human figure.\n\n\n**Refining Framing Parameters**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how to perfectly frame the figure within a container. Keeping the head completely visible is primary, aiming for a small buffer above the hair\'s top. I\'m targeting a cutoff point at or near the elbows. I think setting the top position by either `object-position: top` or a small vertical offset, and then determining the height of the container to hit the elbow region is ideal. The image\'s lower portion will be clipped by the container bounds, making this work, so overflow must be hidden.\n\n\n**Evaluating Container Parameters**\n\nI\'ve been exploring how cropping and scaling affect the figure within the container. Adjusting the zoom to capture from the head (10% height) down to the waist/chest (65% height), results in the elbows getting cut off, or the entire figure being reduced in size! I think a container height of 460px with scaling/offsetting allows the head and torso to be visible, while keeping the elbows in frame.\n\n\n**Calculating Image Cropping**\n\nI\'m now diving into the cropping implications of scaling. A square image at 4000x4000, scaled to 1.4 within a container, results in a cropped view. With `w-full max-w-[540px]` the scaled image is 756px wide, but container is 540px. The difference is cropped. If `origin-top` is set, with a container height of `h-[520px]`, and assuming the head begins 11.3% from the top of the scaled image at approximately 85px from the container top, the bottom of the container is at 68.7% of image height. This results in the container cropping 14.3% from the left and right, due to the container\'s `overflow-hidden`.\n\n\n**Determining Cutting Parameters**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the precise container height for optimal elbow cutoff. I\'m aiming for the head to be fully visible, using a negative translate on the Y-axis if necessary. The ideal container height should truncate the chest/forearm region, ensuring the elbows are outside the bottom. I\'m exploring different scaling and container height combinations to achieve this \"clean\" cutoff.\n\n\n**Optimizing Image Positioning**\n\nI\'m now refining the container\'s vertical positioning to ensure a visually clean elbow cutoff. I\'ve calculated the head\'s position relative to the container height and am now considering negative Y translations to improve the framing.  Adjusting the container height and translation, I\'ve confirmed that a 6% negative translate results in the hair starting approximately 40px below the container\'s top edge. This yields a clean head exposure with the elbows cut off.\n\n\n**Verifying Component References**\n\nI\'m now checking the codebase for hero image usage. Examining `src/components/Hero.tsx`, I\'m confirming the current image framing parameters. Specifically, I see the headroom is optimized and the elbows are consistently cut off. I am reviewing the component to ensure any changes are properly reflected in the visual presentation.\n\n\n**Refining Vertical Adjustments**\n\nI\'ve been examining the effects of `items-end` combined with the Y-axis translations and `object-cover object-top`. The initial setup resulted in clipping the head. Now, I\'m focusing on `items-start` with a reduced container height to correct the framing. I\'m aiming for full head visibility and clean elbow cutoff.\n\n\n**Evaluating Scaling Parameters**\n\nI\'m now analyzing the implications of scaling and translation with this new configuration. The `w-full h-auto aspect-square object-contain object-top scale-[1.4] origin-top translate-y-[-4%]` setup on desktop with `max-w-[520px]`, and `h-[500px]` results in excellent head exposure, with the hair about 35-40px from the top. The image\'s bottom cutoff occurs at approximately 64% of its scaled height, concealing the elbows as desired.\n\n\n"
