    thinking: "**Analyzing Image Dimensions**\n\nI\'m currently focused on image dimensions, specifically the vertical extent of the content. I\'ve noted the hair starts at 5% down and the content ends at 75%. I\'m puzzled, though, as to why the image height extends beyond the visible content, going all the way up to y=3000. It seems the last 25% is empty.\n\n\n**Calculating Image Framing**\n\nI\'m now calculating the ideal framing for the new image, considering its empty space. The hair starts at y=152 (5% of 3000), and I need to ensure it\'s fully visible in the hero section, which has a specific height (e.g., 420px on smaller screens). The bottom 25% is empty. So I\'ll need to figure out how to best position and crop the image to fulfill the requirement of showing the subject\'s head without clipping it, using the given hero section dimensions.\n\n\n**Refining Image Scaling**\n\nI\'m now refining the image scaling and positioning to achieve the desired framing, focusing on achieving a natural cut-off at the bottom. The math checks out: With `scale-[1.3] origin-top translate-y-[-2%]`, the bottom of the subject falls cleanly near the container\'s bottom edge, eliminating awkward empty space or head clipping. I\'ve calculated the pixel values for the hair and content bottom, ensuring they fit within the specified container heights (460px and 490px), verifying clean framing without additional spacing.\n\n\n**Verifying Section Titles**\n\nI\'m now focusing on verifying the section titles and subtitles, as requested. I\'ve begun to enumerate all the sections in `App.tsx`: Services, CaseStudies, WorkingProcess, Team, Testimonials, and ContactSection, along with a footer badge. I am now checking the components for the section headings and subtitles. I\'ve begun inspecting `src/components/Services.tsx`.\n\n\n**Interpreting User Intent**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the user\'s intent regarding section titles and subtitles. I initially thought the \"description\" prop should hold the full subtitle content. However, after re-reading, I realize the user wants me to *number* placeholders like \"Section Title\" and \"Subtitle\" sequentially within the code for easy access later. I\'m focusing on renaming these placeholders appropriately.\n\n\n**Sequencing Section Labels**\n\nI\'m now fully committed to the sequential numbering approach. My conclusion is that using placeholders like \"Section Title 1\" and \"Subtitle 1\" directly in the code will be the clearest solution, allowing for easy updates in subsequent prompts. The user wants the numbered placeholders as keys, anticipating later name changes, not literal display. The goal is to easily find the relevant code sections for title and subtitle modifications.\n\n\n**Evaluating Subtitle Content**\n\nI\'m now evaluating the subtitle content and formatting, focusing on the potential impact of a simplified label. Specifically, I\'m considering whether displaying only \"Subtitle 1\" as the `description` would make the section appear empty, even with the title. I\'ve re-examined `SectionHeading.tsx` to understand the visual hierarchy. My current hypothesis is that the combination of title and badge creates enough visual weight, but a quick test with some dummy data would confirm. I must determine if the placeholders are sufficient.\n\n\n**Interpreting Section Instructions**\n\nI\'m now interpreting the user\'s instructions regarding section title numbering. The user wants the existing \"Section Title\" and subtitle text fields numbered sequentially. If the original subtitle was \"Lorem Ipsum\", the user implies that the new subtitle should also be a placeholder like \"Subtitle 1\". The intention is for easy access, and therefore placeholder titles and subtitles are now necessary.\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Label Placement**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the best placement for the numbered placeholders, considering potential preview visibility. I\'m torn between the simplicity of just \"Subtitle 1\" and the added context of \"Subtitle 1: [original text]\". I\'m now questioning the use case of using the placeholder itself to represent the subtitle. I\'ll need to re-examine the user\'s intent to determine if they want the placeholder shown in a preview or just used for internal identification. I\'m now comparing what would be more clear to a potential end-user: an empty description, or an incomplete description.\n\n\n**Numbering Section Titles**\n\nI\'m now implementing the sequential numbering of section titles and subtitles. My plan is to modify the `SectionHeading.tsx` component to accept numbered placeholders like \"Section Title 1\" and \"Subtitle 1\" as props. This directly addresses the user\'s need for easy access and modification. I\'ll replace the default values with these placeholders to prepare for future updates.\n\n\n**Implementing Placeholder Values**\n\nI\'m now implementing the placeholder values and thinking through the implications for `Hero.tsx`. Considering the \"Section Title 1\" <-> \"Subtitle 1\" pattern, I need to verify that all the sections are correctly numbered with the new labels, starting with the `Hero.tsx` title. I need to make sure that everything stays clear when the new content is added, maintaining easy access. I am now proceeding with the title in the hero section.\n\n\n"
